WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.699
Hi,

00:00:00.699 --> 00:00:01.166
everyone.

00:00:01.166 --> 00:00:02.133
I wonder if you've noticed

00:00:02.133 --> 00:00:03.766
how badly the US dollar has plummeted this year.

00:00:05.066 --> 00:00:06.566
The first half of the year

00:00:06.566 --> 00:00:07.766
was its worst in nearly half a century

00:00:07.766 --> 00:00:08.466
From the beginning of the year to now,

00:00:08.466 --> 00:00:10.466
the US dollar index has fallen by over 10%.

00:00:10.466 --> 00:00:11.699
Don't think 10% isn't a big deal.

00:00:11.699 --> 00:00:12.599
This is the US dollar

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:13.466
not Bitcoin.

00:00:13.466 --> 00:00:15.033
It's the benchmark for global assets.

00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:17.500
This benchmark has shrunk by 10% in six months,

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:19.299
roughly equivalent to everyone growing 10% taller.

00:00:19.300 --> 00:00:19.966
At the same time,

00:00:19.966 --> 00:00:22.333
global asset prices have seen a long-awaited surge,

00:00:22.333 --> 00:00:23.399
with gold being the most dramatic.

00:00:23.399 --> 00:00:24.166
Just look at this chart.

00:00:24.166 --> 00:00:25.833
This shows gold's performance over the past half century.

00:00:25.833 --> 00:00:27.433
I can only describe it as crazy.

00:00:27.433 --> 00:00:28.733
Bitcoin has also hit new highs, which is

00:00:28.733 --> 00:00:29.133
also crazy

00:00:29.133 --> 00:00:30.699
but it has always be crazy.

00:00:30.699 --> 00:00:31.233
Also,

00:00:31.233 --> 00:00:32.399
global stock markets are also rising

00:00:32.399 --> 00:00:35.166
U.S. stocks, European stocks, A-shares, Hong Kong stocks, and Japanese stocks

00:00:35.166 --> 00:00:35.966
are all up .

00:00:37.266 --> 00:00:38.799
Something unusual must be going on

00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:40.199
What is the unusual part?

00:00:40.199 --> 00:00:41.133
Let's take a look at

00:00:41.133 --> 00:00:43.533
the reasons behind the dollar's plunge.

00:00:46.766 --> 00:00:47.199
Today,

00:00:47.199 --> 00:00:48.933
I'm going to talk with you in a more professional

00:00:48.933 --> 00:00:51.366
but at the same time intuitive, and interesting way 

00:00:51.366 --> 00:00:52.600
We talk with charts.

00:00:52.766 --> 00:00:53.766
Let's look at a few charts.

00:00:53.766 --> 00:00:54.833
This is the US dollar index

00:00:54.833 --> 00:00:56.799
and this is the S&amp;P 500 index.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:57.133
You see,

00:00:57.133 --> 00:00:59.399
the stock market and the dollar were highly correlated before

00:00:59.399 --> 00:01:00.633
rising and falling together.

00:01:00.633 --> 00:01:01.366
Look at these two 

00:01:01.366 --> 00:01:02.933
holding hands, happily resonating. 

00:01:02.933 --> 00:01:03.800
But how come in May,

00:01:03.800 --> 00:01:04.566
just when the stock market was halfway through its recovery,

00:01:04.566 --> 00:01:05.166
stock market say

00:01:05.166 --> 00:01:06.133
"Forget it, I don't want to play with you anymore,"

00:01:06.133 --> 00:01:06.966
and ran away

00:01:06.966 --> 00:01:09.266
The US dollar was left alone at the bottom.

00:01:09.266 --> 00:01:10.966
Let me show you another resonance that is even happier.

00:01:10.966 --> 00:01:11.766
This is the resonance between

00:01:11.766 --> 00:01:13.799
 US dollar index and the US 10-year Treasury bond rate.

00:01:13.800 --> 00:01:15.166
They were playing well together before

00:01:15.166 --> 00:01:17.033
but they broke up in May.

00:01:17.033 --> 00:01:18.600
What's more important is the next chart.

00:01:18.600 --> 00:01:20.033
This is the S&amp;P 500 index of US stocks.

00:01:20.033 --> 00:01:22.133
This is a mysterious index .

00:01:22.133 --> 00:01:23.799
The correlation between the two in the past five years

00:01:23.800 --> 00:01:26.266
has been as high as an astonishing 82.4%.

00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:28.799
So why did all these happen?

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:31.033
How does it relate to the US dollar's plunge this year?

00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:32.233
After watching the end of the video today,

00:01:32.233 --> 00:01:33.033
you will understand.

00:01:34.633 --> 00:01:36.033
The first force driving the US dollar down,

00:01:36.033 --> 00:01:37.300
and also the biggest force this year,

00:01:37.300 --> 00:01:38.200
is risk.

00:01:39.199 --> 00:01:39.966
In fact,

00:01:39.966 --> 00:01:42.099
risk has always been a good friend of the US dollar.

00:01:42.100 --> 00:01:44.466
Most of the time, the US dollar is a safe-haven asset. 

00:01:44.466 --> 00:01:45.499
You can see that the US dollar has ushered in a wave of gains

00:01:45.500 --> 00:01:46.566
since September last year.

00:01:46.566 --> 00:01:47.233
Why?

00:01:47.233 --> 00:01:48.600
On the one hand, the US economy

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.100
was indeed relatively strong

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:52.633
but another key factor was global risk aversion,

00:01:52.633 --> 00:01:54.466
especially when everyone saw that Trump was about to be elected

00:01:54.466 --> 00:01:56.166
and who knew how he would mess with other countries.

00:01:56.166 --> 00:01:57.066
Investors thought

00:01:57.066 --> 00:01:57.799
don't make blind guesses,

00:01:57.800 --> 00:01:59.166
holding US dollars will never be wrong, right?

00:01:59.166 --> 00:02:01.000
However, as soon as Trump came to power this year, the intensity of

00:02:01.000 --> 00:02:02.033
his series of tariff policies

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:04.466
far exceeded everyone's expectations.

00:02:04.466 --> 00:02:05.833
At this time, the market reacted.

00:02:05.833 --> 00:02:07.666
Wow, I bought the US dollar to avoid risks 

00:02:07.666 --> 00:02:09.100
but the US dollar is the biggest risk.

00:02:09.100 --> 00:02:10.233
This triggers the US Dollar

00:02:10.233 --> 00:02:12.966
free falling twice in the first half of this year

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:15.100
See this is the US stock VIX

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:16.233
which is also the panic index.

00:02:16.233 --> 00:02:16.966
When people panic,

00:02:16.966 --> 00:02:17.599
the US dollar falls.

00:02:17.600 --> 00:02:18.899
The two major plunges of the US dollar

00:02:18.899 --> 00:02:20.999
were all caused by Trump's tariff-related policies.

00:02:21.000 --> 00:02:22.733
So this year, you can see that in April, 

00:02:22.733 --> 00:02:24.666
it was basically the market's

00:02:24.666 --> 00:02:26.399
concern about the risk of a tariff war or instead the risk of Trump

00:02:26.399 --> 00:02:27.833
And it got even weirder after that.

00:02:27.833 --> 00:02:29.866
We just saw that the interest rate of 10-year US Treasury bonds

00:02:29.866 --> 00:02:32.000
began to break up with the US dollar in May.

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:32.800
Why?

00:02:33.066 --> 00:02:35.966
It's because the market is worried about different risks.

00:02:35.966 --> 00:02:37.566
We have done some videos before to talk about Trump and

00:02:37.566 --> 00:02:38.166
his plans.

00:02:38.166 --> 00:02:40.166
Miran, the chairman of his Council of Economic Advisers,

00:02:40.166 --> 00:02:41.666
specially issued a "Guideline"

00:02:41.666 --> 00:02:42.666
with many radical and 

00:02:42.666 --> 00:02:43.966
contradictory proposals

00:02:43.966 --> 00:02:44.699
He also imagined the

00:02:44.699 --> 00:02:45.833
Mar-a-Lago agreement.

00:02:45.833 --> 00:02:46.499
Later, in fact,

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:48.199
the series of policies introduced by Trump

00:02:48.199 --> 00:02:50.099
were surprisingly similar to this "Guideline".

00:02:50.100 --> 00:02:52.100
Sure enough, he introduced an extremely tough tariff policy

00:02:52.100 --> 00:02:54.199
and tried to interfere with the Federal Reserve's policy.

00:02:54.199 --> 00:02:55.699
As expected, the One Big Beautiful Bill Act was approved.

00:02:55.699 --> 00:02:56.899
everyone's concerns

00:02:56.899 --> 00:02:59.366
are no longer about short-term tariff risks

00:02:59.366 --> 00:03:00.699
but longer-term issues.

00:03:00.699 --> 00:03:01.633
If things continue like this,

00:03:01.633 --> 00:03:02.899
can the U.S. Treasury debt be curbed?

00:03:02.899 --> 00:03:04.299
Can the U.S. government repay its debts?

00:03:04.300 --> 00:03:05.866
Will U.S. inflation get out of control?

00:03:05.866 --> 00:03:06.666
What does this indicate?

00:03:06.666 --> 00:03:07.300
It indicates that the market

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:09.433
is starting to worry about the U.S. government's credit risk

00:03:09.433 --> 00:03:10.966
and is selling off long-term U.S. Treasury bonds,

00:03:10.966 --> 00:03:11.899
causing long-term interest rates to rise.

00:03:11.899 --> 00:03:13.166
This is why we have this chart.

00:03:13.166 --> 00:03:13.966
After May,

00:03:13.966 --> 00:03:15.999
the U.S. dollar and long-term Treasury bond rates "broke up."

00:03:16.866 --> 00:03:18.633
Why were they in resonance before?

00:03:18.633 --> 00:03:19.633
Let me explain.

00:03:19.633 --> 00:03:21.466
If the long-term interest rate of the U.S. dollar rises,

00:03:21.466 --> 00:03:23.366
it means the return on investing in the dollar will increase,

00:03:23.366 --> 00:03:24.666
incentivising people to buy dollars ,

00:03:24.666 --> 00:03:25.666
causing the dollar to rise.

00:03:25.666 --> 00:03:27.766
So, they used to walk hand in hand.

00:03:27.766 --> 00:03:28.366
But this time,

00:03:28.366 --> 00:03:29.433
although the return rate has increased,

00:03:29.433 --> 00:03:30.500
the risk has also increased,

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:31.600
so people are less willing to buy,

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.033
resulting in rising long-term interest rates

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:34.266
and a falling dollar.

00:03:34.266 --> 00:03:35.166
The two broke up.

00:03:35.166 --> 00:03:36.033
I don't know if you're confused,

00:03:36.033 --> 00:03:36.800
but in short, they broke up

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:38.566
because the credit risk of the U.S. government has increased.

00:03:38.566 --> 00:03:39.099
It's like

00:03:39.100 --> 00:03:40.100
a couple

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:42.433
who used to be happy and sad together,

00:03:42.433 --> 00:03:44.266
mainly due to external factors.

00:03:44.266 --> 00:03:45.366
But now, one of them

00:03:45.366 --> 00:03:46.266
starts to act up,

00:03:46.266 --> 00:03:48.266
causing a crisis of trust between the two

00:03:48.266 --> 00:03:49.466
They broke up,

00:03:49.466 --> 00:03:50.566
So here comes the question again.

00:03:50.566 --> 00:03:51.899
Given that the risk of the US dollar is so great and

00:03:51.899 --> 00:03:53.633
the credit risk of US Treasury bonds has become higher,

00:03:53.633 --> 00:03:55.699
so why is the US stock market still soaring?

00:03:55.699 --> 00:03:57.966
Why did the US stock market and the US dollar "break up" again?

00:03:59.500 --> 00:03:59.933
You see,

00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:01.699
although everyone thinks that the US dollar is very risky

00:04:01.699 --> 00:04:02.566
and the US government bonds

00:04:02.566 --> 00:04:03.833
are not as reliable as before

00:04:03.833 --> 00:04:04.899
but US stocks

00:04:04.899 --> 00:04:07.299
and US companies are still very attractive. 

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:09.533
US stocks have indeed continued to rise for many years.

00:04:10.699 --> 00:04:12.533
So even though people don't trust the US government and

00:04:12.533 --> 00:04:13.300
the US dollar so much

00:04:13.300 --> 00:04:14.366
they will still buy US stocks.

00:04:14.366 --> 00:04:15.933
This has led to the continuous rise of US stocks,

00:04:15.933 --> 00:04:18.300
especially AI-related stocks.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.800
In the past, there were stocks like Nvidia, Microsoft, Google, and Meta, and

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.300
recently there has been something like Oracle. 

00:04:23.300 --> 00:04:25.500
It briefly created a new richest man in the world,

00:04:25.500 --> 00:04:26.866
but was later surpassed by Musk.

00:04:26.866 --> 00:04:28.633
We can talk about Oracle later if we have the chance. 

00:04:28.633 --> 00:04:29.366
In short,

00:04:29.366 --> 00:04:31.433
valuations of AI-related stocks are already quite exaggerated.

00:04:32.100 --> 00:04:34.399
So why do foreign capital poured in to invest in US stocks?

00:04:34.399 --> 00:04:36.699
Why is the US dollar not rising but falling?

00:04:38.366 --> 00:04:40.266
The answer behind this is:

00:04:40.266 --> 00:04:41.333
hedging.

00:04:41.466 --> 00:04:42.866
Because the foreign capital that has recently entered

00:04:42.866 --> 00:04:44.933
has begun to hedge the US dollar risk.

00:04:44.933 --> 00:04:45.833
What is hedging the US dollar risk?

00:04:45.833 --> 00:04:46.233
For example

00:04:46.233 --> 00:04:47.100
I am a foreign investor

00:04:47.100 --> 00:04:49.466
and I have a fund of RMB 1 billion.

00:04:49.466 --> 00:04:50.933
Then I want to buy Nvidia

00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:51.633
, for example

00:04:51.633 --> 00:04:53.533
I need to convert these RMB into US dollars

00:04:53.533 --> 00:04:54.300
before buying Nvidia.

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:55.300
In this way,

00:04:55.300 --> 00:04:57.399
I will also bear the foreign exchange risk of the US dollar

00:04:57.399 --> 00:04:58.699
and the stock price risk of Nvidia

00:04:58.699 --> 00:04:59.733
 but now I don't want to bear

00:04:59.733 --> 00:05:01.433
the foreign exchange risk of more than 100 million US dollars,

00:05:01.433 --> 00:05:02.366
I just want to buy Nvidia.

00:05:02.366 --> 00:05:03.200
What should I do?

00:05:03.433 --> 00:05:04.366
I can hedge

00:05:04.366 --> 00:05:06.233
That way, I can short over 100 million US dollars

00:05:06.233 --> 00:05:07.966
in the forex futures, forwards, or swap markets

00:05:07.966 --> 00:05:09.299
while going long on

00:05:09.300 --> 00:05:10.500
1 billion RMB

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:12.633
In this way, I have hedged the foreign exchange risk.

00:05:12.633 --> 00:05:14.999
The demand for the US dollar has not increased.

00:05:15.000 --> 00:05:15.899
Hey, there is no evidence to support

00:05:15.899 --> 00:05:17.366
Let's look at the chart today.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:18.733
You see, this is

00:05:18.733 --> 00:05:20.500
the proportion of hedged and unhedged funds

00:05:20.500 --> 00:05:22.433
flowing into US stock ETFs in the past three years.

00:05:22.433 --> 00:05:23.399
You can clearly see that

00:05:23.399 --> 00:05:25.699
more than 80% of overseas funds

00:05:25.699 --> 00:05:26.533
flowing into US stocks

00:05:26.533 --> 00:05:27.733
did not hedge the US dollar risk

00:05:27.733 --> 00:05:29.966
because the US dollar itself is also an investment product,

00:05:29.966 --> 00:05:31.699
and most of the time it was still quite strong

00:05:31.699 --> 00:05:33.966
but this year it took a 180-degree turn,

00:05:33.966 --> 00:05:36.499
with more than 80% of funds starting to hedge.

00:05:36.566 --> 00:05:38.533
In fact, the resonance between the US dollar and the US stock market

00:05:38.533 --> 00:05:39.399
was not difficult to understand.

00:05:39.399 --> 00:05:40.366
You see, many people who buy US dollars

00:05:40.366 --> 00:05:41.366
will invest in US stocks.

00:05:41.366 --> 00:05:42.399
If you want to buy US stocks ,

00:05:42.399 --> 00:05:43.233
you have to exchange them for US dollars first,

00:05:43.233 --> 00:05:44.600
so the correlation between the two is very high.

00:05:44.600 --> 00:05:46.133
This time, everyone is buying US stocks,

00:05:46.133 --> 00:05:47.633
but hedging the US dollar risk

00:05:47.633 --> 00:05:48.366
and not buying US dollars.

00:05:48.366 --> 00:05:49.433
This is what led to this chart.

00:05:49.433 --> 00:05:50.233
After April,

00:05:50.233 --> 00:05:52.100
the US dollar and the US stock market "broke up"

00:05:52.166 --> 00:05:53.500
just like the couple just now who

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:54.866
had a trust crisis and broke up,

00:05:54.866 --> 00:05:55.333
but

00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:57.233
I still like your piano very much,

00:05:57.333 --> 00:05:58.533
so even though we broke up

00:05:58.533 --> 00:06:00.633
I can still go to your house often to play the piano

00:06:00.633 --> 00:06:01.633
without any interruption.

00:06:01.633 --> 00:06:03.299
I know today's analogy may not be so appropriate,

00:06:03.300 --> 00:06:04.666
Just take it as some light entertainment, guys

00:06:06.433 --> 00:06:07.466
On the one hand ,

00:06:07.466 --> 00:06:08.333
people will flee from the US dollar

00:06:08.333 --> 00:06:10.033
and turn to buy other assets.

00:06:10.033 --> 00:06:10.833
On the other hand,

00:06:10.833 --> 00:06:11.699
the depreciation of the US dollar

00:06:11.699 --> 00:06:12.766
will also drive up the prices of other assets

00:06:12.766 --> 00:06:14.466
from a valuation perspective

00:06:14.466 --> 00:06:14.833
 

00:06:14.833 --> 00:06:17.266
This has, to a certain extent, driven

00:06:17.266 --> 00:06:19.099
up the prices of almost all other assets except the US dollar.

00:06:19.100 --> 00:06:21.300
The most typical example here is gold.

00:06:22.166 --> 00:06:22.633
Think about it,

00:06:22.633 --> 00:06:24.699
the main theme of the financial market this year is risk aversion.

00:06:24.699 --> 00:06:26.699
In the past, risk aversion mainly involved fleeing to US dollar assets

00:06:26.699 --> 00:06:27.666
and Japanese yen assets.

00:06:27.666 --> 00:06:28.466
But now the

00:06:28.466 --> 00:06:29.266
US dollar is not doing well,

00:06:29.266 --> 00:06:30.766
and the Japanese yen is also very volatile

00:06:30.766 --> 00:06:32.866
We will talk about this in the year-end summary. 

00:06:32.899 --> 00:06:33.833
Therefore, the safe-haven assets

00:06:33.833 --> 00:06:34.866
are either the Swiss Franc

00:06:34.866 --> 00:06:35.733
or gold.

00:06:35.733 --> 00:06:36.333
But the Swiss franc

00:06:36.333 --> 00:06:37.533
is very limited after all

00:06:37.533 --> 00:06:38.133
so gold

00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:40.899
has become the first choice for many people in this chaotic world

00:06:40.899 --> 00:06:42.033
Let's again look at the chart.

00:06:42.033 --> 00:06:43.466
This shows the flow of

00:06:43.466 --> 00:06:45.166
gold ETFs in different regions this year.

00:06:45.166 --> 00:06:46.899
It's obvious that there's been a huge influx of funds this year

00:06:46.899 --> 00:06:48.033
and surprisingly,

00:06:48.033 --> 00:06:50.966
the largest influx of funds this year has come from North America.

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:52.699
You should know that in the past, the main demand for gold

00:06:52.699 --> 00:06:53.699
actually came from Asia,

00:06:53.699 --> 00:06:54.333
but this year,

00:06:54.333 --> 00:06:56.499
North America and Europe are also constantly buying.

00:06:56.500 --> 00:06:57.566
But what's interesting is that

00:06:57.566 --> 00:06:58.899
because the price of gold has risen so much,

00:06:58.899 --> 00:07:00.499
it's a bit expensive to make jewelry,

00:07:00.500 --> 00:07:02.433
so the demand for jewelry has begun to decline this year.

00:07:02.433 --> 00:07:04.100
As a result, the total demand for gold

00:07:04.100 --> 00:07:04.966
has not changed much

00:07:04.966 --> 00:07:06.366
but the price has continued to rise.

00:07:06.433 --> 00:07:07.333
Well, we just said that

00:07:07.333 --> 00:07:09.533
the number one driving force behind the plunge in the US dollar this year is risk

00:07:09.633 --> 00:07:10.433
or more so: Trump

00:07:10.433 --> 00:07:12.166
So what is the second biggest driving force?

00:07:12.166 --> 00:07:13.166
You see, in recent months,

00:07:13.166 --> 00:07:15.466
long-term interest rates and US stocks have been out of sync,

00:07:15.466 --> 00:07:17.266
so who's driving the dollar's trend?

00:07:17.266 --> 00:07:19.233
And who's the dollar dating?

00:07:19.266 --> 00:07:20.133
Look at the chart.

00:07:20.133 --> 00:07:21.966
This is the recent trend of the US dollar index

00:07:21.966 --> 00:07:22.566
and this is

00:07:22.566 --> 00:07:24.266
the price of the federal funds rate futures

00:07:24.266 --> 00:07:26.633
expiring in December.

00:07:26.633 --> 00:07:28.066
Something just doesn’t quite add up, does it?

00:07:28.066 --> 00:07:29.933
Let me give it a turn

00:07:30.033 --> 00:07:30.900
 

00:07:31.100 --> 00:07:32.866
This is the new target for the dollar.

00:07:32.866 --> 00:07:33.866
Don't be confused. 

00:07:33.866 --> 00:07:36.533
This is actually the market's expectation of the December interest rate level.

00:07:36.533 --> 00:07:37.133
If you don't know what it means,

00:07:37.133 --> 00:07:39.166
you can check out our first futures video

00:07:39.166 --> 00:07:40.133
To put it bluntly,

00:07:40.133 --> 00:07:41.833
the recent trend of the dollar

00:07:41.833 --> 00:07:44.733
is driven by market expectations of a Fed rate cut.

00:07:44.733 --> 00:07:46.699
That is, I expect the US dollar index to follow the

00:07:46.699 --> 00:07:48.133
changes in the interest rate at the end of the year.

00:07:48.133 --> 00:07:49.866
You see, in the first half of the year, the dollar's trend was driven

00:07:49.866 --> 00:07:50.466
by Trump

00:07:50.466 --> 00:07:51.299
while in the second half

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:52.133
... Powell

00:07:53.300 --> 00:07:55.633
We already looked into this when Powell made his first rate cut

00:07:55.633 --> 00:07:56.299
last year.

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:57.966
The core of his decision to cut interest rates 

00:07:57.966 --> 00:07:59.833
is his dual mandate

00:07:59.833 --> 00:08:01.466
inflation and employment

00:08:01.500 --> 00:08:03.699
In theory, he hopes to suppress inflation to below 2%

00:08:03.699 --> 00:08:04.433
or even lower

00:08:04.433 --> 00:08:05.166
before starting to cut rates

00:08:05.166 --> 00:08:06.266
During this year

00:08:06.266 --> 00:08:07.166
some core indicators,

00:08:07.166 --> 00:08:08.333
whether CPI

00:08:08.333 --> 00:08:09.566
or core PCE,

00:08:09.566 --> 00:08:11.066
have basically been hovering around 3%.

00:08:11.066 --> 00:08:12.666
Although it is not the most ideal state

00:08:12.666 --> 00:08:13.733
it is acceptable.

00:08:13.733 --> 00:08:15.633
Therefore, the Fed's main decision-making focus

00:08:15.633 --> 00:08:16.933
is on employment data.

00:08:16.933 --> 00:08:17.299
In other words,

00:08:17.299 --> 00:08:19.299
he wants to see weaker employment data

00:08:19.299 --> 00:08:19.766
before cutting rates

00:08:19.766 --> 00:08:21.499
The Fed's control of monetary policy

00:08:21.500 --> 00:08:22.233
is like

00:08:22.233 --> 00:08:23.266
 a class teacher

00:08:23.266 --> 00:08:24.433
who has to manage a class of students.

00:08:24.433 --> 00:08:25.833
He has two KPIs

00:08:25.833 --> 00:08:27.233
he wants students to have good grades

00:08:27.233 --> 00:08:28.833
and to keep their bodies healthy

00:08:28.833 --> 00:08:29.566
the class teacher

00:08:29.566 --> 00:08:30.899
can only control one thing

00:08:30.899 --> 00:08:32.433
which is the amount of homework.

00:08:32.433 --> 00:08:34.333
This is like the Fed can only control interest rates

00:08:34.333 --> 00:08:35.733
If more homework is given,

00:08:35.733 --> 00:08:36.666
the grades may go up,

00:08:36.666 --> 00:08:37.699
but the body cannot handle

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:38.766
If less homework is given,

00:08:38.766 --> 00:08:39.966
the grades may go down,

00:08:39.966 --> 00:08:41.333
but the body may recover a little

00:08:41.333 --> 00:08:41.933
becomes better.

00:08:41.933 --> 00:08:42.733
I'm just giving an analogy,

00:08:42.733 --> 00:08:43.633
please don't take it too seriously.

00:08:43.633 --> 00:08:44.399
Look,

00:08:44.399 --> 00:08:45.133
the situation faced by

00:08:45.133 --> 00:08:46.699
the Fed and the head teacher

00:08:46.700 --> 00:08:47.700
is actually a bit like

00:08:47.700 --> 00:08:49.100
the goals of inflation and employment.

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:50.733
Often, it really is a case of one falling as the other rises.

00:08:50.733 --> 00:08:51.899
 In the past one or two years,

00:08:51.899 --> 00:08:53.666
the grades of these students are pretty good.

00:08:53.666 --> 00:08:54.833
Although they cannot be said to be excellent

00:08:54.833 --> 00:08:55.733
they all score more than 80 points.

00:08:55.733 --> 00:08:57.500
The head teacher has recently paid special attention

00:08:57.500 --> 00:08:59.099
to their physical health.

00:08:59.100 --> 00:08:59.866
If I find

00:08:59.866 --> 00:09:02.066
everyone's health shows signs of decline,

00:09:02.066 --> 00:09:03.366
I'll start reducing the amount of homework.

00:09:03.366 --> 00:09:04.566
But if I don't see

00:09:04.566 --> 00:09:05.966
any health decline,

00:09:05.966 --> 00:09:06.766
and everyone is healthy and

00:09:06.766 --> 00:09:07.466
doing well academically

00:09:07.466 --> 00:09:08.500
I can't reduce the amount of homework.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:10.033
Otherwise, everyone might be too energetic

00:09:10.033 --> 00:09:10.933
and go out to play

00:09:10.933 --> 00:09:13.033
and if they play too much, they might even damage their health.

00:09:13.033 --> 00:09:14.166
Then their academic performance will be bad

00:09:14.166 --> 00:09:14.866
and that's even worse . 

00:09:14.866 --> 00:09:16.433
This is economic "stagflation."

00:09:16.433 --> 00:09:17.633
Students might also be in stagnation

00:09:18.133 --> 00:09:19.733
a clever pun for everyone.

00:09:21.066 --> 00:09:22.033
Anyway, that's what I mean .

00:09:22.033 --> 00:09:22.766
The class teacher

00:09:22.766 --> 00:09:24.633
keeps an eye on the students' health indicators every month,

00:09:24.633 --> 00:09:26.733
and the Fed keeps an eye on the employment data every month

00:09:26.733 --> 00:09:27.966
When the data gets bad enough, 

00:09:27.966 --> 00:09:29.033
immediately cut interest rates.

00:09:29.033 --> 00:09:29.966
What's the actual situation?

00:09:29.966 --> 00:09:32.433
In the first half of this year, it was average, but also OK.

00:09:32.433 --> 00:09:34.066
In addition, Trump's policies

00:09:34.066 --> 00:09:35.133
has very high uncertainty

00:09:35.133 --> 00:09:36.833
so the Fed cut interest rates until halfway.

00:09:36.833 --> 00:09:37.899
And in recent months,

00:09:37.899 --> 00:09:39.633
you can see that this is the expected interest rate in December.

00:09:39.633 --> 00:09:41.833
There have been big jumps at the beginning of the month for these three months

00:09:41.833 --> 00:09:42.933
This is actually due to

00:09:42.933 --> 00:09:44.933
the market reaction after the release of 

00:09:44.933 --> 00:09:45.933
the NFP non-farm payroll data. 

00:09:45.966 --> 00:09:47.466
The July employment data was not bad.

00:09:47.466 --> 00:09:49.433
Everyone thought that the Fed might not cut the rates much

00:09:49.433 --> 00:09:50.533
so the interest rate jumped up.

00:09:50.533 --> 00:09:52.133
The August employment data was very bad.

00:09:52.133 --> 00:09:52.933
You can see that the interest rate

00:09:52.933 --> 00:09:53.933
dropped suddenly

00:09:53.933 --> 00:09:55.433
This data is very critical

00:09:55.433 --> 00:09:56.766
because it is really too bad.

00:09:56.766 --> 00:09:57.933
It seems that the students in my class

00:09:57.933 --> 00:09:59.099
have started to feel dizzy 

00:09:59.100 --> 00:09:59.899
and vomit.

00:09:59.899 --> 00:10:02.299
The class teacher has to immediately reduce the amount of homework.

00:10:02.299 --> 00:10:03.766
So in August, after that data,

00:10:03.766 --> 00:10:05.299
the market was almost certain

00:10:05.299 --> 00:10:06.566
the Fed would cut interest rates in September.

00:10:06.566 --> 00:10:08.433
Then, the September data wasn't very good,

00:10:08.433 --> 00:10:09.733
and interest rates fell further.

00:10:09.733 --> 00:10:10.633
And the US dollar index

00:10:10.633 --> 00:10:12.299
basically fluctuated along with

00:10:12.299 --> 00:10:13.033
the interest rate

00:10:13.033 --> 00:10:15.333
So, the market had already fully anticipated

00:10:15.366 --> 00:10:16.899
the Fed's rate cut. 

00:10:16.899 --> 00:10:18.099
When the Fed actually cut,

00:10:18.100 --> 00:10:20.133
the market reaction wasn't exactly minimal,

00:10:20.133 --> 00:10:21.566
but rather unremarkable.

00:10:22.399 --> 00:10:23.533
I actually want to discuss

00:10:23.533 --> 00:10:25.099
the NFP non-farm payroll data.

00:10:25.100 --> 00:10:26.366
There's something very interesting

00:10:26.366 --> 00:10:28.466
and even a bit strange about it lately

00:10:28.466 --> 00:10:29.733
It's so inaccurate.

00:10:29.733 --> 00:10:31.633
I don't know if you've seen similar news before,

00:10:31.633 --> 00:10:33.699
but the recent revisions are truly exaggerated.

00:10:34.433 --> 00:10:36.233
This data is called NFP

00:10:36.233 --> 00:10:38.100
Non-Farm Payroll. 

00:10:38.100 --> 00:10:38.666
In simple terms, 

00:10:38.666 --> 00:10:40.733
it tells us how much employment grew each month

00:10:40.733 --> 00:10:41.733
excluding sectors like agriculture, 

00:10:41.733 --> 00:10:43.966
self-employment, and so on.

00:10:43.966 --> 00:10:46.100
For example, if the survey results show 100,000 in one month,

00:10:46.100 --> 00:10:46.666
this means that

00:10:46.666 --> 00:10:48.366
the non-farm payroll population in the US 

00:10:48.366 --> 00:10:50.033
increased by 100,000 in that month .

00:10:50.033 --> 00:10:50.733
In theory,

00:10:50.733 --> 00:10:52.066
if the Fed wants to see the employment situation more accurately, 

00:10:52.066 --> 00:10:53.366
it must be the unemployment rate. 

00:10:53.366 --> 00:10:54.433
However, the problem with the unemployment rate is

00:10:54.433 --> 00:10:56.166
that it fluctuates too little from month to month and

00:10:56.266 --> 00:10:56.933
is not sensitive enough.

00:10:56.933 --> 00:10:58.766
Moreover, it also depends on the participation rate

00:10:58.766 --> 00:11:00.366
that is, how many people want to find a job,

00:11:00.366 --> 00:11:01.500
which is obviously very subjective

00:11:01.500 --> 00:11:02.299
and more difficult to judge.

00:11:02.299 --> 00:11:02.966
Non-farm payroll data

00:11:02.966 --> 00:11:03.733
is just a number,

00:11:03.733 --> 00:11:04.833
which is relatively more objective

00:11:04.833 --> 00:11:06.033
and much easier to measure.

00:11:06.033 --> 00:11:06.466
It's like

00:11:06.466 --> 00:11:08.299
a class teacher wants to understand everyone's health status.

00:11:08.299 --> 00:11:10.033
In theory, you should have a physical examination 

00:11:10.033 --> 00:11:11.166
and a psychological assessment monthly.

00:11:11.166 --> 00:11:12.366
But if you have to take the test every month, 

00:11:12.366 --> 00:11:13.366
it is obviously too expensive,

00:11:13.366 --> 00:11:14.966
so I can settle for the second best

00:11:14.966 --> 00:11:17.700
and simply look at average sleep time of each student.

00:11:17.700 --> 00:11:19.299
Although it isn't the best indicator

00:11:19.299 --> 00:11:20.233
but it can be used to roughly

00:11:20.233 --> 00:11:21.666
track everyone's health.

00:11:21.666 --> 00:11:23.933
I might then do a full physical at the end of the year.

00:11:23.933 --> 00:11:24.966
The non-farm payroll data

00:11:24.966 --> 00:11:26.533
is a bit like the class teacher

00:11:26.533 --> 00:11:27.566
checking sleep time.

00:11:27.566 --> 00:11:28.633
But what's so outrageous about it?

00:11:28.633 --> 00:11:30.666
The NFP, which seems to be the most accurate,

00:11:30.666 --> 00:11:31.666
has seen significant revisions

00:11:31.666 --> 00:11:33.066
for several consecutive months.

00:11:33.066 --> 00:11:34.100
For example, the May employment data

00:11:34.100 --> 00:11:36.299
was released on June 6th, showing an increase of 139,000

00:11:36.299 --> 00:11:36.700
which was OK.

00:11:36.700 --> 00:11:39.100
Then it was revised on July 3rd to 144,000,

00:11:39.100 --> 00:11:40.066
5,000 more than the original.

00:11:40.066 --> 00:11:41.533
The market felt that it was better, but

00:11:41.533 --> 00:11:43.699
then it was revised again on August 1st.

00:11:43.700 --> 00:11:44.466
Sorry,

00:11:44.466 --> 00:11:45.299
I was careless.

00:11:45.299 --> 00:11:46.499
I was wrong for the past two months. 

00:11:46.500 --> 00:11:47.466
It was not 144,000

00:11:47.466 --> 00:11:48.700
but 19,000.

00:11:49.433 --> 00:11:51.266
The June employment data was also similar.

00:11:51.266 --> 00:11:52.833
The first release said it was 147,000

00:11:52.833 --> 00:11:53.933
and then it was revised on August 1st to say, "

00:11:53.933 --> 00:11:54.366
Sorry,

00:11:54.366 --> 00:11:55.433
it's not 147,000."

00:11:55.433 --> 00:11:56.166
It was 14,000,

00:11:56.166 --> 00:11:57.533
and then the next month, again

00:11:57.533 --> 00:11:58.033
Sorry,

00:11:58.033 --> 00:11:59.299
it didn't increase by 14,000

00:11:59.299 --> 00:12:00.766
but decreased by 13,000.

00:12:00.766 --> 00:12:02.433
Don't you think this revision is outrageous?

00:12:02.433 --> 00:12:03.233
I even suspect

00:12:03.233 --> 00:12:04.733
that there was a decimal point mistake

00:12:04.733 --> 00:12:06.633
That's why we saw the market plummet

00:12:06.633 --> 00:12:07.633
on August 1st

00:12:07.633 --> 00:12:09.299
because it suffered a triple blow that day. 

00:12:09.299 --> 00:12:10.933
Not only was the newly released

00:12:10.933 --> 00:12:12.533
July data worse than expected,

00:12:12.533 --> 00:12:14.699
the June data was revised down by more than 130,000,

00:12:14.700 --> 00:12:16.899
and the May data was revised down by more than 120,000.

00:12:16.899 --> 00:12:18.733
So a few hours after this number came out,

00:12:18.733 --> 00:12:19.533
Trump was furious.

00:12:19.533 --> 00:12:21.866
He said, "Your Census Bureau is deliberately embarrassing me

00:12:21.866 --> 00:12:23.266
and you are manipulating the data."

00:12:23.266 --> 00:12:25.233
He immediately fired the director of the Bureau of Labor Statistics.

00:12:25.233 --> 00:12:26.133
How could this data

00:12:26.133 --> 00:12:27.466
be so inaccurate?

00:12:27.466 --> 00:12:29.700
The point is that it is such an important data.

00:12:29.700 --> 00:12:30.833
The most important interest rate in the world

00:12:30.833 --> 00:12:31.899
depends on U.S. employment.

00:12:31.899 --> 00:12:33.333
U.S. employment depends on this  number

00:12:33.333 --> 00:12:34.433
It determines

00:12:34.433 --> 00:12:35.933
the trend of the global capital market.

00:12:35.933 --> 00:12:36.933
And then every month

00:12:36.933 --> 00:12:38.433
my guess is probably more accurate than this

00:12:38.433 --> 00:12:39.899
so many people may have conspiracy theories about

00:12:39.899 --> 00:12:41.299
whether this data is manipulated.

00:12:41.299 --> 00:12:42.299
I don't know about this

00:12:42.299 --> 00:12:43.299
but I can explain

00:12:43.299 --> 00:12:45.666
some possible reasons

00:12:45.666 --> 00:12:47.033
why there is such a large correction in theory.

00:12:47.033 --> 00:12:48.466
This part may be more theoretical.

00:12:48.466 --> 00:12:49.233
If you are not interested,

00:12:49.233 --> 00:12:50.133
you can skip to this part.

00:12:51.233 --> 00:12:52.500
First of all, the volume of this correction

00:12:52.500 --> 00:12:53.966
is not as large as everyone feels,

00:12:53.966 --> 00:12:55.633
because this number is a variable.

00:12:55.633 --> 00:12:57.266
The total non-farm payroll population in the United States

00:12:57.266 --> 00:12:58.866
is about 159.5 million.

00:12:58.866 --> 00:12:59.833
Your increase of 100,000

00:12:59.833 --> 00:13:01.899
is actually equivalent to 0.06%.

00:13:01.899 --> 00:13:03.633
In fact, corrections of more than 100,000

00:13:03.633 --> 00:13:04.366
are common in history

00:13:04.366 --> 00:13:06.500
Why does it feel so obvious recently?

00:13:06.500 --> 00:13:08.299
Because the data was relatively good before.

00:13:08.299 --> 00:13:09.966
For example, if there is an increase of 300,000 jobs per month,

00:13:09.966 --> 00:13:11.566
then if you reduce it by 100,000, it will become 200,000,

00:13:11.566 --> 00:13:13.266
a decrease of 1/3. Although it is not a small amount

00:13:13.266 --> 00:13:14.133
but it is acceptable.

00:13:14.133 --> 00:13:14.833
But recently, 

00:13:14.833 --> 00:13:16.066
it has increased by 110,000.

00:13:16.066 --> 00:13:17.866
Then you lowered it by 100,000 to 10,000. 

00:13:17.866 --> 00:13:18.766
It is also a reduction of 100,000.

00:13:18.766 --> 00:13:20.366
but you have lowered it by 90%.

00:13:20.366 --> 00:13:21.333
Psychologically,

00:13:21.333 --> 00:13:22.533
you feel that it has been adjusted a lot.

00:13:22.533 --> 00:13:23.499
This is one of the reasons.

00:13:23.500 --> 00:13:24.333
However, 

00:13:24.333 --> 00:13:26.500
the extent of its revision has increased significantly recently,

00:13:26.500 --> 00:13:27.633
and it is indeed more inaccurate.

00:13:27.633 --> 00:13:29.133
This requires us to talk about the non-farm payroll data.

00:13:29.133 --> 00:13:30.133
How is it calculated ?

00:13:30.933 --> 00:13:32.466
It mainly relies on questionnaires

00:13:32.466 --> 00:13:33.766
They ask 120,000 companies

00:13:33.766 --> 00:13:35.699
how many employees they have in the past month. 

00:13:35.700 --> 00:13:36.666
What is the problem?

00:13:36.666 --> 00:13:37.566
After the pandemic,

00:13:37.566 --> 00:13:39.666
companies have been submitting questionnaires more and more slowly.

00:13:39.666 --> 00:13:41.733
It is actually impossible to submit

00:13:41.733 --> 00:13:44.266
all 120,000 company questionnaires immediately in the same month.

00:13:44.266 --> 00:13:44.833
Therefore, generally, in

00:13:44.833 --> 00:13:47.033
the first month, a data is announced based on 

00:13:47.033 --> 00:13:47.999
the number of questionnaires already received.

00:13:48.000 --> 00:13:48.633
In the next two months,

00:13:48.633 --> 00:13:50.266
as the questionnaires are collected little by little,

00:13:50.266 --> 00:13:52.466
the data will be revised.

00:13:52.466 --> 00:13:53.333
You see, this is the historical

00:13:53.333 --> 00:13:55.266
recovery rate of questionnaires in three months.

00:13:55.266 --> 00:13:55.766
In the first month,

00:13:55.766 --> 00:13:57.466
before the pandemic, 70 to 80% could be recovered.

00:13:57.466 --> 00:13:59.333
After the pandemic, it is now less than 60%.

00:13:59.333 --> 00:13:59.833
Therefore,

00:13:59.833 --> 00:14:02.133
the accuracy of the number obtained in the first month

00:14:02.133 --> 00:14:03.233
will be significantly reduced.

00:14:03.233 --> 00:14:03.866
Moreover, even if

00:14:03.866 --> 00:14:05.766
all 120,000 questionnaires are collected,

00:14:05.766 --> 00:14:06.666
It's just a sample.

00:14:06.666 --> 00:14:08.899
A comprehensive survey must be conducted every year

00:14:08.899 --> 00:14:10.666
and then the annual data must be revised.

00:14:10.666 --> 00:14:12.299
In September they just completed

00:14:12.299 --> 00:14:14.099
annual non-farm employment data 

00:14:14.100 --> 00:14:16.266
from April 2024 to March 2025

00:14:16.266 --> 00:14:18.866
The data for the entire year was actually revised down by 910,000

00:14:18.866 --> 00:14:20.766
which is the largest downward revision in history.

00:14:22.166 --> 00:14:23.166
The official explanation is

00:14:23.166 --> 00:14:24.499
some model adjustment or something.

00:14:24.500 --> 00:14:25.700
Some people have conspiracy theories.

00:14:25.700 --> 00:14:27.100
Some people say it's an immigration issue.

00:14:27.100 --> 00:14:28.633
I won't analyse it here

00:14:28.633 --> 00:14:29.299
I dont know it.

00:14:29.299 --> 00:14:30.466
But what's interesting is that

00:14:30.466 --> 00:14:32.233
this time the annual data was revised so much, but

00:14:32.233 --> 00:14:34.433
Trump didn't get angry 

00:14:34.433 --> 00:14:35.366
Why is that?

00:14:35.366 --> 00:14:37.433
